---
/**
 * Layer 1 of the insurance analyser: the verdict, and the two numbers it rests
 * on. Rewritten 18 Aug for the overhaul.
 *
 * The single comparison this whole subject reduces to (dd-005) is: keep the
 * policy, or buy the cover on its own and invest the difference? Everything
 * else on this page is a push on one side of it.
 *
 * The design note that matters is in `InsuranceAnswer.checks.md`: the replica
 * is DEFINED to fund the identical income in the identical years, so the income
 * is held constant by construction and the only thing left to compare is what
 * remains at the end. That is said on the screen, in those words — without it a
 * reader cannot tell whether the surplus was bought by cutting their income.
 *
 * WHAT CHANGED IN THE OVERHAUL
 *
 *  - THE RATE IS SAID IN THE READER'S WORDS. "TRUE YIELD (XIRR)" was F-13's
 *    finding and it led the old page. What a reader wants to know is what the
 *    policy pays them back a year, after tax, and that is what it now says.
 *
 *  - THE TAX-FREE LINE IS NEW, and it is the policy's genuine advantage. Where
 *    s.10(10D) exempts the proceeds, the policy's rate is worth more than the
 *    same rate from a bond, and almost nothing else on the internet will tell a
 *    policyholder that. Where the exemption is LOST, the same line says so.
 *    dd-021: a tool willing to print only one of those two is advocacy.
 *
 *  - F-12 / dd-019. Every figure names what it counts on the same line as the
 *    amount, or sits under a column header that frames it. "THE SAME MONEY" is
 *    gone — it read as a claim of equivalence when it meant "this sum,
 *    re-expressed".
 *
 * Every figure here is rendered by insurance-analyzer.astro from the one
 * `analyseReplication` result. This component holds no state and reads no form.
 */
---

<section
  id="insuranceAnswer"
  class="rounded-2xl border border-navy-200 dark:border-navy-800 bg-white dark:bg-navy-950 p-6 sm:p-8"
  aria-live="polite"
>
  <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-4 border-b border-navy-100 dark:border-navy-800 pb-4 mb-6">
    <h2 class="text-sm font-semibold tracking-tight text-navy-600 dark:text-navy-300">
      Keep this policy, or do it yourself?
    </h2>
    <span
      id="answerBadge"
      class="shrink-0 px-2 py-0.5 text-xs font-bold tracking-tight rounded bg-navy-100 dark:bg-navy-800 text-navy-600 dark:text-navy-400"
      >Working it out</span
    >
  </div>

  <!--
    dd-008: the values live in fixed slots the eye can return to, and the prose
    around them stays true at every position of every input. Nothing here is
    rewritten as a control moves; only the slots change.
  -->
  <p
    id="answerHeadline"
    class="text-lg sm:text-xl leading-relaxed text-navy-900 dark:text-navy-100"
  >
    Tell us about the policy and this will say which way the arithmetic goes.
  </p>

  <!--
    The premise the comparison rests on. Fixed text on purpose: it is a
    principle, not a state (dd-008), and without it the figures below are
    unreadable — the reader has no way to know the surplus was not bought by
    cutting their own income.
  -->
  <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-navy-600 dark:text-navy-400 leading-relaxed">
    Both routes pay you <strong class="font-semibold text-navy-900 dark:text-navy-100"
      >the same money, at the same times</strong
    >, and after the same taxes. The do-it-yourself route is built to fund every rupee this
    policy hands you, so the only things left to compare are what is left over at the end and
    what the protection costs to keep.
    <!--
      "the same money, at the same times" rather than "the same income, in the
      same years": this is fixed text and must stay true at every input
      (dd-008/dont-1), and an endowment holder has no income and one year. The
      headline above carries the shape-specific wording, because it is a slot
      that changes with the answer rather than a principle that does not.
    -->
  </p>

  <!--
    T8 / dd-004 / dd-019. One quantity, two moneys, adjacent columns with the
    frame in the header and the quantity in the row. That is the arrangement
    dd-019's own note names as passing, and it is the fix for F-12: the phrase
    "in today's prices" now appears once, over a column, rather than four times
    beside four different figures.
  -->
  <div class="mt-6 overflow-x-auto">
    <table class="w-full text-left border-collapse">
      <thead>
        <tr class="border-b border-navy-200 dark:border-navy-800">
          <th class="py-2 pr-3 text-sm font-semibold text-navy-600 dark:text-navy-400"></th>
          <th class="py-2 px-3 text-right text-sm font-semibold text-navy-600 dark:text-navy-400">
            in the rupees of that year
          </th>
          <th class="py-2 pl-3 text-right text-sm font-semibold text-navy-600 dark:text-navy-400">
            in today's prices
          </th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr class="border-b border-navy-100 dark:border-navy-900">
          <th
            id="answerFigureLabel"
            scope="row"
            class="py-3 pr-3 text-sm font-semibold text-navy-900 dark:text-navy-100 align-baseline"
          >Left over at the end</th>
          <td class="py-3 px-3 text-right align-baseline">
            <span id="answerFigureNominal" class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-extrabold tracking-tight tabular-nums text-navy-900 dark:text-navy-100">₹0</span>
          </td>
          <td class="py-3 pl-3 text-right align-baseline">
            <span id="answerFigureReal" class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-extrabold tracking-tight tabular-nums text-navy-900 dark:text-navy-100">₹0</span>
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row" class="py-3 pr-3 text-sm font-semibold text-navy-900 dark:text-navy-100 align-baseline">
            What the same protection costs to buy on its own
          </th>
          <td class="py-3 px-3 text-right align-baseline">
            <span id="answerCoverCost" class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-extrabold tracking-tight tabular-nums text-navy-900 dark:text-navy-100">₹0</span>
          </td>
          <td class="py-3 pl-3 text-right align-baseline">
            <span id="answerCoverCostReal" class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-extrabold tracking-tight tabular-nums text-navy-900 dark:text-navy-100">₹0</span>
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p id="answerCoverNote" class="mt-3 text-sm text-navy-600 dark:text-navy-400 leading-relaxed">&nbsp;</p>

  <!--
    THE RATE, in the reader's words. The old page led with "TRUE YIELD (XIRR)"
    in a stat block, which is the answer to an analyst's question. This is the
    answer to theirs — and the tax line beside it is the policy's real
    advantage, printed whether or not it applies.
  -->
  <div class="mt-6 rounded-xl border border-navy-200 dark:border-navy-800 bg-navy-50 dark:bg-navy-900 p-5 space-y-2">
    <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-navy-600 dark:text-navy-400">
      What this policy pays you back, a year, after tax
    </p>
    <p class="text-3xl font-extrabold tracking-tight tabular-nums text-navy-900 dark:text-navy-100">
      <span id="answerYield">0.00%</span>
    </p>
    <p id="answerYieldNote" class="text-sm text-navy-600 dark:text-navy-400 leading-relaxed">&nbsp;</p>
    <p id="answerTaxNote" class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-navy-600 dark:text-navy-400 empty:hidden"></p>
  </div>

  <!--
    dd-017. A level premium is a ten-year commitment quoted in flat rupees, so
    it gets quietly cheaper every year — which flatters the policy. The page
    never said so. It does now.
  -->
  <p id="answerPremiumDecay" class="mt-5 text-sm text-navy-600 dark:text-navy-400 leading-relaxed empty:hidden">
  </p>
</section>
